\section*{Rokhlin multiple-mixing problem}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
Every invertible probability-preserving mixing transformation is claimed to be mixing at every finite order. The cited manuscript argues for that result; its relation to this problem is specified below.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Mathematical claim 1.} On any probability space $(X,\mathcal F,\mu)$, suppose $T$ is invertible, bimeasurable, measure preserving and mixing in the two-set sense. OpenAI claims that for every $k\ge3$ and fixed measurable $A_1,\ldots,A_k$,
\[
\mu\bigl(A_1\cap T^{-n_1}A_2\cap\cdots\cap T^{-(n_1+\cdots+n_{k-1})}A_k\bigr)
\longrightarrow\prod_{j=1}^k\mu(A_j)
\]
as the minimum of the positive integer gaps $n_1,\ldots,n_{k-1}$ tends to infinity. No countable-generation or standardness assumption is made.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 145, 2026-09-23): A hypothetical nonproduct joining yields moving labels coupled to a zero-entropy process. Dissociated label orbits are canceled by exponential estimates against subexponential word counts, while polynomial label orbits are reduced in degree by Hilbert-space averaging; ordinary mixing anchors the reduction and contradicts the preserved positive correlation.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Manuscript 1 (family 145, 2026-09-23): For the customary invertible Rokhlin mixing problem, the theorem establishes every finite order and hence the recorded order-three assertion; it imposes no quantitative mixing rate or standardness assumption.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 145, the scope page documents the following selections. The selected implication gives mixing of every finite order for one invertible probability-preserving mixing map, with arbitrary measurable-set inputs and divergent successive time gaps. The documented statement covers the main claim of manuscript 1. Its selected declaration also covers the clause used for this problem. Comparator files: Rokhlin.lean. Declarations: OAI.Rokhlin.mixing\_all\_finite\_orders.

This site has not executed the Lean files or checked their dependencies. These descriptions report the scope of the linked declarations, rather than a new verification of the proofs.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these results to an internal model. Its release notice cautions that results lacking full formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently assessed the proofs; a solved label records the scope of the reported claim.

\subsection{Sources}
